**Title:** Predictive Forecasting of Care Load & Placement Demand

## 1. Introduction

Government healthcare and child-welfare systems operate in a high-uncertainty environment where sudden changes in migration trends, policy enforcement, and humanitarian conditions can significantly impact the number of children requiring federal care.
Traditional descriptive analytics only explains historical patterns. However, decision-makers require predictive intelligence to anticipate future care demand, discharge capacity, and system pressure.

This project develops a predictive forecasting dashboard to estimate future care load, discharge demand, and capacity risk using time-series and machine-learning models. The system enables proactive planning and resource allocation for healthcare administrators.

---

## 2. Dataset Description

The dataset contains daily operational metrics of children in federal care.

**Columns used:**

* Date – Reporting date
* Children apprehended and placed in CBP custody – Daily intake volume
* Children in CBP custody – Active CBP care load
* Children transferred out of CBP custody – Flow into HHS system
* Children in HHS Care – Active care load (target variable)
* Children discharged from HHS Care – Successful placements

The dataset is time-series based and recorded daily, enabling forecasting of future trends.

---

## 3. Data Preparation

Data cleaning and preprocessing steps included:

* Converted date column to datetime index
* Sorted values chronologically
* Removed duplicate rows
* Cleaned numeric columns by removing commas and special characters
* Converted all metrics to numeric format
* Ensured daily continuity using time-series frequency alignment
* Handled missing values using interpolation and forward/backward fill

This ensured the dataset was structured and suitable for forecasting models.

---

## 4. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

EDA was performed to understand patterns and relationships:

* Trend analysis of care load over time
* Intake vs discharge comparison
* Seasonal weekly patterns
* Net pressure indicator (intake − discharge)
* Capacity risk distribution

Key findings:

* Care load follows stable but trend-driven patterns
* Intake and discharge directly impact future care load
* System pressure increases when intake exceeds discharge
* No critical capacity breach observed in recent period

---

## 5. Analytical Approach

### Feature Engineering

To improve forecasting accuracy, the following predictive features were created:

* Lag features (1-day, 7-day, 14-day)
* Rolling averages (7-day and 14-day)
* Rolling standard deviation
* Net pressure indicator
* Calendar features (day of week, month)

These features capture trend, seasonality, and system pressure.

### Model Development

Multiple forecasting models were implemented:

**Baseline models:**

* Naive forecast

**Statistical model:**

* SARIMA (Seasonal ARIMA)

**Machine learning model:**

* Random Forest Regressor

Models were evaluated using time-based train-test split.

### Evaluation Metrics

* MAE (Mean Absolute Error)
* RMSE (Root Mean Square Error)
* MAPE (Mean Absolute Percentage Error)

---

## 6. Dashboard Implementation

An interactive Streamlit dashboard was developed with:

* Forecast horizon selection
* Model selection
* KPI display (MAE, RMSE, MAPE)
* Intake vs discharge trends
* Net pressure indicator
* Capacity risk monitoring
* Model comparison table

Plotly visualizations were used for dynamic charts.

---

## 7. Results & Insights

### Model Performance

Random Forest achieved highest accuracy:

* MAPE below 1%
* Stable long-range forecasting
* Captured trend and seasonal patterns effectively

SARIMA also performed well but slightly less accurate.

### Operational Insights

* Care load trends are predictable using historical patterns
* Intake and discharge strongly influence future load
* Early forecasting enables proactive planning
* Machine learning models outperform baseline methods

---

## 8. Business Recommendations

* Use predictive dashboard for weekly planning
* Scale shelters and staff based on forecast trends
* Monitor net pressure indicator daily
* Increase discharge processing during surge periods
* Integrate forecasting into decision-making workflow

---

## 9. Conclusion

This project successfully transforms historical operational data into predictive intelligence. By combining time-series forecasting and machine learning with an interactive dashboard, healthcare planners can anticipate demand, reduce overcrowding risk, and improve resource allocation.

The system demonstrates how data-driven forecasting can enhance government healthcare planning and operational efficiency.
